s Meyer Driscoll was the U.S. Navy's chief cryptanalyst for three decades, breaking Japanese naval codes and training generations of American codebreakers. Known as 'Miss Aggie' and 'Madame X', she was the most formidable cryptanalyst in prewar America.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers","codebreaker biography"]},{"t":"Alberti Cipher Disk","u":"ciphers/alberti-disk.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Renaissance · 1467","b":"Broken","s":"Alberti cipher disk — Leon Battista Alberti's 1467 rotating disk, the first true polyalphabetic device and the tool that began the cipher revolution.","g":["renaissance","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Ethiopian Geʼez Monastic Ciphers","u":"ciphers/amharic-ge-ez-ciphers.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"East Africa · 14th–19th c.","b":"Historical (low security)","s":"Ethiopian Orthodox monasteries used syllabary-based substitution ciphers in Ge'ez script to protect sacred texts and inter-monastery communications from the 14th century onward.","g":["medieval","world origins of cryptography","historical (low security)"]},{"t":"Arabic Nomenclators","u":"ciphers/arabic-nomenclators.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Abbasid Caliphate · ~850 CE","b":"Broken (by its own inventors)","s":"9th-century Abbasid Baghdad — al-Kindi's Risalah and the codebooks of the Arab cryptographic golden age, where frequency analysis was born.","g":["medieval","world origins of cryptography","broken (by its own inventors)"]},{"t":"Argenti Family Cipher","u":"ciphers/argenti.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Renaissance Italy · 1500s–1600s","b":"Secure for its time · obsoleted by frequency analysis","s":"Matteo and Marcello Argenti served as papal cryptanalysts to seven popes. Their <em>Trattato in Cifra</em> codified the homophonic-nomenclator pattern that dominated European diplomacy for two centuries.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","secure for its time · obsoleted by frequency analysis"]},{"t":"Arnold–André Book Cipher","u":"ciphers/arnold-andre.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"American Revolution · 1779–1780","b":"Compromised by capture (1780)","s":"The book cipher Benedict Arnold and Major John André used to plot the surrender of West Point. Each plaintext word located in a shared book (Blackstone’s <em>Commentaries</em>) and transmitted as the triple page.line.word.","g":["modern","military & spy ciphers","compromised by capture (1780)"]},{"t":"Atbash","u":"ciphers/atbash.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Ancient · ~500 BC","b":"Trivial","s":"Atbash — the ancient Hebrew reflection cipher (A<->Z, B<->Y, C<->X), self-inverse and used in the Book of Jeremiah (25:26, 51:41) for SHESHACH.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","trivial"]},{"t":"Autokey","u":"ciphers/autokey.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Frontier","c":"Renaissance · 1586","b":"Statistical (Friedman, 1920s)","s":"Autokey (1586) — Vigenère's self-extending key, the message becomes its own key. Effectively unbreakable until Friedman's 1920s statistical attack.","g":["renaissance","polyalphabetic frontier","statistical (friedman, 1920s)"]},{"t":"The Babington Plot Cipher","u":"ciphers/babington.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Elizabethan · 1586","b":"Broken in days · Mary executed 1587","s":"Babington Plot nomenclator (1586) — the cipher that condemned Mary, Queen of Scots, broken in days by Walsingham's cryptographer Thomas Phelippes.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","broken in days · mary executed 1587"]},{"t":"Bach's B-A-C-H Motif","u":"ciphers/bach-motif.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Baroque Germany · 1750","b":"Musical cryptogram","s":"The four notes B♭ – A – C – B♮ spell 'BACH' in German musical notation — a self-signature Bach embedded in his final unfinished fugue.","g":["modern","cipher culture","musical cryptogram"]},{"t":"Bacon's Cipher","u":"ciphers/bacon.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"1605","b":"Broken","s":"Bacon's bilateral cipher (1605) — Francis Bacon's 5-bit binary code that hides letters inside a two-typeface carrier, the direct ancestor of ASCII.","g":["renaissance","puzzle & novelty ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Bazeries Cipher","u":"ciphers/bazeries.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"1890s · France","b":"Broken","s":"Bazeries cylinder cipher — Etienne Bazeries' 1891 wheel cipher with 20 alphabet disks, an evolution later adopted by the US Army as the M-94 in 1922.","g":["19th century","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Beale Ciphers","u":"ciphers/beale.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"19th Century · published 1885","b":"Unsolved (Beale 1 & 3)","s":"Beale ciphers — three numerical ciphers published in an 1885 Lynchburg pamphlet, said to have been written in the 1820s and to point to a Virginia treasure. Only #2 was solved (Declaration of Independence key); #1 and #3 unsolved.","g":["19th century","unsolved ciphers","unsolved (beale 1 & 3)"]},{"t":"Beaufort Cipher","u":"ciphers/beaufort.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"Victorian · 1857","b":"Broken","s":"Beaufort cipher (1857) — Sir Francis Beaufort's reciprocal Vigenère variant where encrypt and decrypt are the same operation. Broken by Kasiski examination.","g":["19th century","polyalphabetic revolution","broken"]},{"t":"Arne Beurling","u":"ciphers/beurling.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Sweden","b":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Swedish mathematician Arne Beurling broke the German Siemens T52 Geheimschreiber cipher in two weeks in 1940 — an achievement described by NSA historians as perhaps the greatest individual cryptanalytic feat in history.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers","codebreaker biography"]},{"t":"Bible Code — Equidistant Letter Sequences","u":"ciphers/bible-code.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"1994 · Refuted 1999","b":"Debunked","s":"The Bible Code — equidistant letter sequences (ELS) claimed to hide prophecies in the Hebrew scriptures. A rigorous statistical refutation by McKay et al. (1999) showed the same method finds identical 'predictions' in War and Peace and Moby-Dick.","g":["digital","puzzle & novelty ciphers","debunked"]},{"t":"Bifid Cipher","u":"ciphers/bifid.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"1901","b":"Broken","s":"Bifid cipher (1901) — Felix Delastelle's fractionation cipher that splits Polybius coordinates, transposes them across a period, and recombines.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Bill Tutte","u":"ciphers/bill-tutte.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Bill Tutte was a Cambridge mathematician who reconstructed the complete logical structure of the Lorenz SZ40 cipher machine from ciphertext alone — without ever seeing the machine — enabling the creation of Colossus, the world's first programmable electronic computer.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers","codebreaker biography"]},{"t":"Bletchley Park","u":"ciphers/bletchley-park.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · 1939–1945","b":"Historical","s":"Bletchley Park (Station X) was the British WWII signals intelligence headquarters where 10,000 people broke Enigma, Lorenz, and dozens of other Axis ciphers — arguably shortening the war by two years.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers","historical"]},{"t":"Book Cipher","u":"ciphers/book-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Field","c":"18th–20th c.","b":"Secure if the book is unguessable; broken otherwise","s":"Book Cipher (18th c.) — if both correspondents share the same book, the book itself is the key. Used by Benedict Arnold, Aaron Burr, and Beale.","g":["19th century","military & field","secure if the book is unguessable; broken otherwise"]},{"t":"Cabinet Noir — Black Chambers of Europe","u":"ciphers/cabinet-noir.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Europe · 1550–1850","b":"Historical","s":"The European Black Chambers (cabinets noirs) were government departments that intercepted, deciphered, and resealed diplomatic mail from the 16th through 19th centuries — proto-signals-intelligence agencies.","g":["renaissance","hall of codebreakers","historical"]},{"t":"Caesar Cipher","u":"ciphers/caesar.html","k":"cipher","h":"Hall I","c":"Ancient Rome","b":"Broken","s":"Caesar cipher — the simplest substitution cipher, used by Julius Caesar around 58 BC with a shift of 3. Broken by Al-Kindi's frequency analysis circa 850 AD.","g":["ancient","hall i","broken"]},{"t":"Cardano Autokey","u":"ciphers/cardano-autokey.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Polyalphabetic Era","c":"Italian Renaissance · 1550","b":"Easy (recovery cascade)","s":"Girolamo Cardano's 1550 autokey — prime the keystream with a single seed letter, then continue it with the plaintext itself. The first published self-keying scheme.","g":["renaissance","the polyalphabetic era","easy (recovery cascade)"]},{"t":"Cardano Grille","u":"ciphers/cardano-grille.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Transposition Hall","c":"Italian Renaissance · 1550","b":"Weak (steganography, not encryption)","s":"Girolamo Cardano's 1550 grille — a card with cut-out windows. Write the secret through the windows; fill the rest with innocuous text; the recipient overlays an identical grille to read.","g":["renaissance","the transposition hall","weak (steganography, not encryption)"]},{"t":"Chaocipher","u":"ciphers/chaocipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Modern · 1918","b":"Strong (until disclosed)","s":"Chaocipher (1918) — John F. Byrne's two-disk dynamic cipher that permutes both alphabets after every character. Algorithm kept secret for 92 years until the 2010 disclosure; never cryptanalytically broken.","g":["wwi","mechanical cipher machines","strong (until disclosed)"]},{"t":"Che Guevara's VIC Variant","u":"ciphers/che-guevara.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1956–1967","b":"Broken (capture of materials)","s":"Ernesto 'Che' Guevara used a modified VIC straddling-checkerboard cipher for guerrilla communications in Bolivia and the Congo, 1956–1967.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","broken (capture of materials)"]},{"t":"Chinese Telegraph Code","u":"ciphers/chinese-telegraph.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Late Qing → PRC · 1881–1980s","b":"Public codebook (super-enciphered when secret)","s":"The 1881 Chinese Telegraph Code mapped 7,000+ Chinese characters to 4-digit code groups so they could ride the same Morse-style telegraph network as the Latin alphabet — and was super-enciphered for diplomatic and military traffic in the 20th century.","g":["modern","civil war gallery","public codebook (super-enciphered when secret)"]},{"t":"Cicada 3301","u":"ciphers/cicada-3301.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Internet era · 2012–2014","b":"Multi-stage ARG / recruitment puzzle","s":"An anonymous 2012–2014 internet recruitment puzzle that combined steganography, classical ciphers, GPS coordinates on physical posters, and a copy of the Liber Primus hand-illustrated in glyphic runes.","g":["digital","cipher culture","multi-stage arg / recruitment puzzle"]},{"t":"Columnar Transposition","u":"ciphers/columnar.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","s":"Columnar transposition — write plaintext under a keyword, then read columns in keyword order. Broken by Friedman-era multiple-anagram methods in the 1920s.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Commercial Telegraph Codebooks","u":"ciphers/commercial-codebooks.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Victorian → inter-war · 1870s–1930s","b":"Public codebooks (compression, not secrecy)","s":"Commercial telegraph codebooks (ABC Code, Bentley’s, Lieber’s) were the workhorses of Victorian and Edwardian global business. Each English word or phrase mapped to a 5-letter pronounceable codeword — cheaper, denser, and incidentally a layer of confidentiality.","g":["modern","civil war gallery","public codebooks (compression, not secrecy)"]},{"t":"Confederate Vigenère","u":"ciphers/confederate-vigenere.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Confederate · 1862","b":"Broken","s":"Confederate Vigenère (1861-1865) — the brass cipher disk used by Confederate officers, broken in 1863 once the three repeating field keys were recovered.","g":["wwi","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Copiale Cipher","u":"ciphers/copiale.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty","c":"18th Century · ~1760s","b":"Broken (2011)","s":"Copiale Cipher — the 105-page 18th-century Oculist Order manuscript with homophonic substitution. Broken in 2011 by Knight, Megyesi, and Schaefer using HMM.","g":["renaissance","puzzle & novelty","broken (2011)"]},{"t":"Culper Ring / Tallmadge Code","u":"ciphers/culper-ring.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"American Revolution · 1778–1783","b":"Codebook (never captured, never broken)","s":"Major Benjamin Tallmadge’s 1779 codebook for George Washington’s Culper Spy Ring. Roughly 760 numbered entries covering people, places, military terms, and common words — used by Abraham Woodhull and Robert Townsend on British-occupied Long Island and Manhattan.","g":["modern","military & spy ciphers","codebook (never captured, never broken)"]},{"t":"The Da Vinci Code","u":"ciphers/da-vinci-code.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Fiction · 2003","b":"Pop Culture","s":"Dan Brown's 2003 thriller made cryptography mainstream — Atbash, the Fibonacci sequence, and anagrams as plot devices that introduced millions to cipher thinking.","g":["digital","cipher culture","pop culture"]},{"t":"D'Agapeyeff Cipher","u":"ciphers/dagapeyeff.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Pre-WWII Britain · 1939","b":"Unsolved challenge cipher","s":"A 1939 textbook 'cryptogram for the student' that the author himself later admitted he had forgotten how to solve.","g":["wwii","unsolved ciphers","unsolved challenge cipher"]},{"t":"Conan Doyle's Dancing Men","u":"ciphers/dancing-men.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Edwardian Britain · 1903","b":"Fictional substitution cipher","s":"The fictional stick-figure cipher Sherlock Holmes solves in The Adventure of the Dancing Men (1903) — a frequency-analysis primer disguised as a Strand magazine story.","g":["modern","cipher culture","fictional substitution cipher"]},{"t":"DES (Data Encryption Standard)","u":"ciphers/des.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1977","b":"Broken (brute force, 1998)","s":"DES (1977) — the first public, government-standardised cipher; broken by EFF Deep Crack in 1998 and the spark of modern academic cryptanalysis.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","broken (brute force, 1998)"]},{"t":"Diana Cryptosystem","u":"ciphers/diana-cryptosystem.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1960s–1990s","b":"Unbroken (correct use)","s":"The US Army Special Forces one-time pad field system (1960s–1990s) — trigraph-based OTP cards issued to Green Berets in Vietnam.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","unbroken (correct use)"]},{"t":"Confederate Dictionary Code","u":"ciphers/dictionary-code.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Confederate · 1862","b":"Broken","s":"Dictionary code — the Civil War book cipher that encodes each word as page-and-word-number pairs keyed to a shared dictionary, immune to frequency attacks.","g":["wwi","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange","u":"ciphers/diffie-hellman.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1976","b":"Secure (with 2048+ bit groups)","s":"Diffie-Hellman (1976) — two strangers create a shared secret over an open wire, ending three thousand years of must-meet-first cryptography.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","secure (with 2048+ bit groups)"]},{"t":"Dilly Knox","u":"ciphers/dilly-knox.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWI/WWII · Britain","b":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Alfred Dillwyn \"Dilly\" Knox was the British codebreaker who solved the Enigma wiring before Turing, broke the Abwehr Enigma, and was the first to crack the Italian commercial Enigma variant.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers","codebreaker biography"]},{"t":"Dorabella Cipher","u":"ciphers/dorabella.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"19th c. · 1897","b":"Unsolved","s":"Dorabella Cipher (1897) — Edward Elgar's 1897 cryptogram — 87 squiggles that have resisted every attempt since. Hall XII of The Cipher Museum.","g":["19th century","unsolved ciphers","unsolved"]},{"t":"Double Transposition","u":"ciphers/double-transposition.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"WWI · Germany","b":"Weak","s":"Double transposition — apply columnar transposition twice with two keys. The WWII Allied workhorse, considered unbreakable until Lasry et al. (2013).","g":["wwi","transposition & fractionation","weak"]},{"t":"Elonka Dunin","u":"ciphers/dunin.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Modern · Cryptographer","b":"Living Legend","s":"Elonka Dunin is a game designer, cryptographer, and the foremost public authority on unsolved ciphers — the creator of the definitive list of famous unsolved codes and an expert on Kryptos.","g":["modern","hall of codebreakers","living legend"]},{"t":"ECDSA","u":"ciphers/ecdsa.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1992","b":"Secure (with a sound nonce)","s":"ECDSA (1992) — signing with elliptic curves, and the one-line mistake that handed Sony's PlayStation 3 master key to the world.","g":["digital","modern cryptography","secure (with a sound nonce)"]},{"t":"Egyptian Substitution Hieroglyphs","u":"ciphers/egyptian-substitution.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Middle Kingdom Egypt · ~1900 BCE","b":"Decorative concealment","s":"The Khnumhotep II tomb inscription (~1900 BCE) — the world's earliest surviving instance of a deliberate substitution cipher, using unusual hieroglyph variants.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","decorative concealment"]},{"t":"Elizebeth Smith Friedman","u":"ciphers/elizebeth-friedman.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Interwar & WWII · USA","b":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Elizebeth Smith Friedman was America's first female professional cryptanalyst, who broke rum-runner codes during Prohibition, exposed Nazi spy networks in South America, and laid the foundations for what became the NSA.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers","codebreaker biography"]},{"t":"Enigma Machine","u":"ciphers/enigma.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"WWII · 1918–1945","b":"Broken","s":"Enigma (1923) — the German rotor cipher securing Wehrmacht and Kriegsmarine traffic. Broken by Polish mathematician Marian Rejewski in 1932.","g":["wwii","the unbreakable","broken"]},{"t":"Fialka M-125","u":"ciphers/fialka.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Cold War · 1956","b":"Never broken (in service)","s":"The Soviet Cold-War rotor machine. Ten rotors, half stepping forward and half backward, plus a punch-card key sheet — the answer to every weakness Soviet analysts saw in Enigma.","g":["cold war","mechanical cipher machines","never broken (in service)"]},{"t":"Field Hollers and Coding Songs","u":"ciphers/field-hollers.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Americas · 1619–1865","b":"Historical (social steganography)","s":"American enslaved workers used field hollers, work songs, and coded idiom as covert communication — \"Follow the Drinking Gourd\" encoding an escape route along the Underground Railroad.","g":["modern","cipher culture","historical (social steganography)"]},{"t":"Four-Square Cipher","u":"ciphers/four-square.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Late 19th Century · ~1902","b":"Moderate","s":"Four-Square cipher (1902) — Delastelle's digram cipher using two keyed and two standard 5x5 squares. Disrupts Playfair frequencies but breaks to digrams.","g":["19th century","classical substitution","moderate"]},{"t":"Fractionated Morse Cipher","u":"ciphers/fractionated-morse.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","s":"Fractionated Morse — converts text to Morse, regroups dots, dashes, and separators in trigrams, then substitutes via a keyed alphabet. Broken by trigram stats.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Freemason Pigpen Tradition","u":"ciphers/freemason-pigpen.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Early modern Europe · 17th–19th c.","b":"Fraternal symbol-substitution alphabet","s":"The geometric pigpen alphabet — fragments of grids and X-shapes — used by Freemasons, Rosicrucians, and the Hellfire Club from the 17th century onwards as a fraternal cipher and ritual marker.","g":["modern","cipher culture","fraternal symbol-substitution alphabet"]},{"t":"The GCHQ Trio — Ellis, Cocks, and Williamson","u":"ciphers/gchq-trio.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Cold War · GCHQ","b":"Invention Biography","s":"James Ellis, Clifford Cocks, and Malcolm Williamson of GCHQ independently invented public-key cryptography in 1970–1973 — but their work remained classified until 1997, some twenty years after Diffie, Hellman, and Rivest had received public credit.","g":["cold war","hall of codebreakers","invention biography"]},{"t":"Geheimschreiber T52 (Sturgeon)","u":"ciphers/geheimschreiber.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWII · 1932–1945","b":"Broken (Beurling, 1940)","s":"The Siemens T52 “Sturgeon” — a German WWII teleprinter cipher with ten irregularly-clocked wheels feeding an additive keystream and a per-character permutation. Broken by Arne Beurling and the Swedish FRA in summer 1940.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken (beurling, 1940)"]},{"t":"Poe's Gold-Bug Cipher","u":"ciphers/gold-bug.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Antebellum America · 1843","b":"Fictional substitution cipher","s":"Edgar Allan Poe's 1843 short story The Gold-Bug — the first widely read piece of fiction to teach cryptanalysis, complete with a worked decipherment of a buried-treasure cryptogram.","g":["modern","cipher culture","fictional substitution cipher"]},{"t":"Gravity Falls Cipher System","u":"ciphers/gravity-falls.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Animation · 2012–2016","b":"Pop Culture","s":"The Disney animated series Gravity Falls (2012–2016) hid a different cipher in every episode's credits — Caesar, Atbash, A1Z26 numeric, Vigenère — teaching real cryptography to a generation of children.","g":["digital","cipher culture","pop culture"]},{"t":"The Great Cipher","u":"ciphers/great-cipher.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Baroque · ~1626","b":"—","s":"Great Cipher of Louis XIV (1626) — the Rossignol father-and-son syllabic nomenclator with delete-previous-letter traps. Broken by Bazeries in 1893.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution"]},{"t":"Gronsfeld Cipher","u":"ciphers/gronsfeld.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"17th Century","b":"Broken","s":"Gronsfeld cipher (1655) — a Vigenère variant restricted to numeric keys (0-9), making it field-portable but with a smaller key space that falls fast to Kasiski.","g":["renaissance","polyalphabetic revolution","broken"]},{"t":"Hill Cipher","u":"ciphers/hill.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"1929","b":"Broken","s":"Hill cipher (1929) — Lester S. Hill's matrix-based polygraphic cipher, first to apply linear algebra to encryption. Broken by known-plaintext (mod-26 system).","g":["interwar","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Histiaeus's Tattooed Messenger","u":"ciphers/histiaeus-tattoo.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Classical Greece · ~499 BCE","b":"Steganography (concealment)","s":"Histiaeus of Miletus (~499 BCE) — the Greek tyrant who tattooed a secret revolt order on a slave's shaved scalp, then waited for the hair to regrow before sending him.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","steganography (concealment)"]},{"t":"Homophonic Substitution","u":"ciphers/homophonic.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Renaissance · ~1400","b":"Weak","s":"Homophonic substitution (1400s) — replace each letter with one of several numeric codes to flatten frequencies. Defeated Mary in 1586; Great Cipher in 1893.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","weak"]},{"t":"IRA Cipher Traffic","u":"ciphers/ira-ciphers.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Ireland · 1926–1928","b":"Broken (key text identifiable)","s":"About 1,300 IRA cryptograms from the papers of chief of staff Moss Twomey, mostly 1926–1927, enciphered by columnar transposition and broken almost in their entirety by Jim Gillogly with hill-climbing software — published as Decoding the IRA (2008).","g":["interwar","military & spy ciphers","broken (key text identifiable)"]},{"t":"Jefferson Disk","u":"ciphers/jefferson-disk.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"1795 (Jefferson) · 1922 (M-94)","b":"Broken","s":"Jefferson disk cipher (1795) — Thomas Jefferson's 36-disk wheel cipher, rediscovered and adopted by the US Army as the M-94 in 1922. Solved by the late 20th c.","g":["interwar","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"JN-25","u":"ciphers/jn-25.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"World War II - 1939-1945","b":"Broken in depth","s":"JN-25 (1939-1945) - the Imperial Japanese Navy's superenciphered codebook system broken by Station HYPO and central to Midway intelligence.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken in depth"]},{"t":"Joan Clarke","u":"ciphers/joan-clarke.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Joan Clarke was a Bletchley Park cryptanalyst who worked in Hut 8 alongside Alan Turing, making crucial contributions to breaking the Naval Enigma and to the development of the Bombe.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers","codebreaker biography"]},{"t":"David Kahn","u":"ciphers/kahn.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Modern · Journalist/Historian","b":"Cryptohistory Biography","s":"David Kahn's 1967 masterwork The Codebreakers was the first comprehensive history of cryptography — a book so thorough that the NSA tried to suppress its publication.","g":["modern","hall of codebreakers","cryptohistory biography"]},{"t":"Kama Sutra Cipher","u":"ciphers/kama-sutra.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Classical India · ~400 CE","b":"Trivial","s":"The Mlecchita Vikalpa cipher from Vatsyayana's Kama Sutra (~400 CE) — a paired-letter substitution taught to lovers as one of the 64 arts.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","trivial"]},{"t":"Kerckhoffs's Principle","u":"ciphers/kerckhoffs.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Foundational · 1883","b":"Principle (not a cipher)","s":"Auguste Kerckhoffs's 1883 principle — 'A cipher should be secure even if everything about the system, except the key, is public knowledge' — is the bedrock of modern cryptographic design.","g":["19th century","modern cryptography","principle (not a cipher)"]},{"t":"KL-7 ADONIS","u":"ciphers/kl-7.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Cold War · 1952","b":"Never broken cryptanalytically","s":"The KL-7 (ADONIS) was NATO’s standard cipher machine from 1952 to 1968. Eight rotors, seven of which stepped, on a re-entrant alphabet. Compromised by John Walker, not by cryptanalysis.","g":["cold war","mechanical cipher machines","never broken cryptanalytically"]},{"t":"Kryha","u":"ciphers/kryha.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Inter-war commercial · 1924","b":"Broken (Friedman, 1933)","s":"Alexander von Kryha’s 1924 pocket cipher — a clockwork-driven mixed alphabet wheel sold to banks and diplomats as unbreakable, then broken in two hours by William Friedman.","g":["interwar","mechanical cipher machines","broken (friedman, 1933)"]},{"t":"ARG Ciphers","u":"ciphers/krypto-arg.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Web 2.0 · 2001–present","b":"Promotional / collaborative cipher hunts","s":"Alternate-reality games — from The Beast (2001) to Year Zero (2007) and beyond — built on the assumption that players will recognise, transcribe, and crack classical ciphers across forums, phone calls, and physical artifacts.","g":["digital","cipher culture","promotional / collaborative cipher hunts"]},{"t":"Kryptos","u":"ciphers/kryptos.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Modern · 1990","b":"K1–K3 Broken · K4 Unsolved","s":"Kryptos (1990) — Jim Sanborn's CIA-courtyard sculpture in four sections. K1, K2, K3 solved by 1999 (keyed Vigenère and transposition); K4 still unsolved.","g":["digital","unsolved ciphers","k1–k3 broken · k4 unsolved"]},{"t":"George Lasry","u":"ciphers/lasry.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Modern · Computational","b":"Living Legend","s":"George Lasry is a computational cryptographer who has solved more historically unsolved cipher manuscripts in the past decade than anyone alive — including the Copiale Cipher and multiple Zodiac Killer messages.","g":["modern","hall of codebreakers","living legend"]},{"t":"Latin American Telegraphic Codebooks","u":"ciphers/latin-american-codebooks.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Americas · 1870s–1940s","b":"Low (commercial, not military)","s":"The commercial telegraph boom of 1870–1940 spawned a rich family of Latin American codebooks — from the Código Comercial Mexicano to the Código Telegráfico Argentino — that shaped 20th-century trade.","g":["modern","mechanical cipher machines","low (commercial, not military)"]},{"t":"Leo Marks","u":"ciphers/leo-marks.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Leo Marks was the SOE's head of cryptography in WWII, who replaced vulnerable poem codes with one-time pads for field agents, dramatically reducing agent capture rates. He wrote the poem 'The Life That I Have' for agent Violette Szabo.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers","codebreaker biography"]},{"t":"Lorenz Cipher","u":"ciphers/lorenz.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"WWII · Germany · 1940","b":"Broken","s":"Lorenz SZ40 cipher (1940) — the German High Command teleprinter cipher with twelve pin-wheels. Broken by Bill Tutte in 1942; industrialised by Colossus in 1944.","g":["wwii","the unbreakable","broken"]},{"t":"M-94 / CSP-488","u":"ciphers/m-94.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWI → WWII tactical · 1922–1943","b":"Tactical only","s":"The M-94 (Army) and CSP-488 (Navy) — 25 brass disks on a spindle, each carrying a mixed alphabet. Jefferson’s 1795 idea, mass-produced for WWI tactical traffic and used into 1942.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","tactical only"]},{"t":"M-209 (Hagelin C-38)","u":"ciphers/m209.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"World War II · 1940","b":"Strong (tactical)","s":"M-209 cipher machine (1940) — the US Army's portable Hagelin C-38 with six co-prime pin-wheels and a 27-bar lug cage. Broken in the field in WWII.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","strong (tactical)"]},{"t":"Mary Queen of Scots — The Castelnau Letters","u":"ciphers/mary-stuart-castelnau-letters.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"1586","b":"Solved 2022 — after 436 years","s":"The 1586 Castelnau letters by Mary Queen of Scots — a 50,000-character cipher solved by George Lasry, Norbert Biermann, and Satoshi Tomokiyo in 2022, revealing undocumented French diplomatic intelligence.","g":["renaissance","hall of codebreakers","solved 2022 — after 436 years"]},{"t":"Mavis Batey","u":"ciphers/mavis-batey.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Mavis Batey (née Lever) was a Bletchley Park cryptanalyst who broke the Italian naval Enigma in 1941, providing the intelligence behind the Battle of Cape Matapan — the Royal Navy's greatest WWII Mediterranean victory.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers","codebreaker biography"]},{"t":"McCormick Notes","u":"ciphers/mccormick.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Late 20th-c. United States · 1999","b":"Unsolved homicide-evidence cipher","s":"Two handwritten notes found in the pockets of murder victim Ricky McCormick in 1999 — declared 'unbreakable' by the FBI's Cryptanalysis and Racketeering Records Unit in 2011.","g":["digital","unsolved ciphers","unsolved homicide-evidence cipher"]},{"t":"Microdot Steganography","u":"ciphers/microdot.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Modern · 1941–1980s","b":"Broken (FBI discovers technique)","s":"The microdot — a photograph reduced to the size of a typographic period — was the most effective covert communication tool of the 20th century, used by the Abwehr, KGB, and CIA from WWII through the Cold War.","g":["wwii","military & spy ciphers","broken (fbi discovers technique)"]},{"t":"MIT Mystery Hunt","u":"ciphers/mit-mystery-hunt.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"MIT · 1981–present","b":"Annual collaborative puzzle hunt","s":"An annual three-day puzzle marathon at MIT (since 1981) where teams solve dozens of layered cryptographic, linguistic, and lateral-thinking puzzles to find a hidden coin somewhere on campus.","g":["cold war","cipher culture","annual collaborative puzzle hunt"]},{"t":"Monoalphabetic Substitution","u":"ciphers/monoalphabetic.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Medieval · ~800 AD","b":"Broken","s":"Monoalphabetic substitution — replace each letter with a fixed substitute. Foundation of all substitution ciphers; broken by Al-Kindi's frequency analysis ~850.","g":["medieval","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Morse Code","u":"ciphers/morse.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Telegraph age · 1840s →","b":"Encoding, not encryption","s":"International Morse code — the dot-dash telegraph alphabet that made the wired world possible and provided the carrier signal for a century of military and amateur ciphers.","g":["victorian","world origins of cryptography","encoding, not encryption"]},{"t":"National Treasure","u":"ciphers/national-treasure.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Fiction · 2004","b":"Pop Culture","s":"The 2004 Disney film National Treasure sent audiences hunting for cipher clues on the US dollar bill, the Liberty Bell, and Independence Hall — all real historical sites.","g":["digital","cipher culture","pop culture"]},{"t":"Navajo Code Talkers","u":"ciphers/navajo-code-talkers.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWII · 1942–1945","b":"Never Broken","s":"Navajo Code (1942-1945) — a constructed military vocabulary spoken by 420 Marine Code Talkers. Never broken in wartime; declassified in 1968 after long use.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","never broken"]},{"t":"Nihilist Cipher","u":"ciphers/nihilist.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"1880s · Russia","b":"Broken","s":"Nihilist cipher (1880s) — the Russian revolutionary classic adding Polybius numbers to a repeating keyword's Polybius numbers by plain addition. Broken early 1900s.","g":["19th century","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Nomenclator","u":"ciphers/nomenclator.html","k":"cipher","h":"Substitution Era","c":"Renaissance · 1400s–1800s","b":"Statistical + intercept-volume","s":"Nomenclator (1400s) — The 400-year diplomatic compromise: a small substitution alphabet plus a large code dictionary. Hall II of The Cipher Museum.","g":["renaissance","substitution era","statistical + intercept-volume"]},{"t":"Null Cipher","u":"ciphers/null-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Transposition Hall","c":"Antiquity → present","b":"Weak (concealment, not encryption)","s":"The null cipher — hide the secret in plain sight by spelling it out with the first letters of an innocuous message. Used by spies, prisoners, and Bacon, since at least the Roman empire.","g":["ancient","the transposition hall","weak (concealment, not encryption)"]},{"t":"One-Time Pad","u":"ciphers/one-time-pad.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"1882 · Theoretical","b":"Provably Secure*","s":"One-Time Pad (Miller 1882 / Mauborgne 1917) — the only cipher mathematically unbreakable when the key is truly random, key-length, and never reused (Shannon).","g":["theoretical","the unbreakable","provably secure*"]},{"t":"Patterson's Cipher for Jefferson","u":"ciphers/patterson-jefferson-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"1801","b":"Solved 2007 — after 206 years","s":"Robert Patterson's 1801 cipher for Thomas Jefferson — text written down the lines, cut into sections of at most nine, the lines shuffled and padded with arbitrary letters. Unread for 206 years until Lawren Smithline solved it in 2007 by dynamic programming over digraph frequencies.","g":["modern","classical substitution","solved 2007 — after 206 years"]},{"t":"Phaistos Disc","u":"ciphers/phaistos-disc.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Bronze Age Crete · ~1700 BCE","b":"Undeciphered script","s":"A 1700 BCE Cretan terracotta disc stamped with 45 distinct pictograms — the world's oldest 'movable type' and one of its most enduring undeciphered scripts.","g":["ancient","unsolved ciphers","undeciphered script"]},{"t":"Pigpen Cipher","u":"ciphers/pigpen.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"18th Century","b":"Broken","s":"Pigpen cipher (~1700) — Freemason geometric substitution where each letter becomes the lines and dots of its grid cell. Trivial once the glyph table is seen.","g":["medieval","puzzle & novelty ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Playfair Cipher","u":"ciphers/playfair.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Victorian · 1854","b":"Broken","s":"Playfair cipher (1854) — Charles Wheatstone's keyed 5x5 digram cipher promoted by Lord Playfair. Used by the British in WWI; broken by digram analysis.","g":["19th century","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Polybius Square","u":"ciphers/polybius.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Ancient Greece · ~150 BC","b":"Broken","s":"Polybius square (~150 BC) — ancient Greek 5x5 grid mapping each letter to a row-column digit pair. Foundation for Bifid, Trifid, Nihilist, ADFGX, and tap codes.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Porta Cipher","u":"ciphers/porta.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"Renaissance · 1563","b":"Broken","s":"Porta cipher (1563) — della Porta's reciprocal polyalphabetic table where each key letter swaps two halves of the alphabet. Falls to Kasiski just like Vigenère.","g":["renaissance","polyalphabetic revolution","broken"]},{"t":"Purple (97-shiki ōbun injiki)","u":"ciphers/purple.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"World War II · 1939–1945","b":"Broken (1940)","s":"Purple machine (1939) — Japan's Type 97 stepping-switch cipher for diplomatic traffic. Broken on 20 September 1940 by Frank Rowlett and the US Army SIS team.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken (1940)"]},{"t":"Rail Fence Cipher","u":"ciphers/rail-fence.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","s":"Rail Fence cipher — write the message in a zigzag across N rails, then read off rail by rail. Trivial to break: brute-force every rail count from 2 to about 10.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Red Army Faction One-Time Pad","u":"ciphers/red-army-faction.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1970–1998","b":"Unbroken (pads not recovered)","s":"The West German Red Army Faction (RAF/Baader-Meinhof) used KGB-supplied one-time pads for communications — materials discovered in 1993 after German reunification archive access.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","unbroken (pads not recovered)"]},{"t":"Red (Type A)","u":"ciphers/red-type-a.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Interwar to WWII - 1931-1938","b":"Broken by SIS","s":"Red (Type A) (1931-1938) - the Japanese diplomatic stepping-switch predecessor to Purple and the cryptanalytic bridge to MAGIC.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken by sis"]},{"t":"Joseph Rochefort","u":"ciphers/rochefort.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Pacific","b":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Commander Joseph Rochefort led Station HYPO and broke JN-25 in 1942, providing the intelligence that won the Battle of Midway and turned the tide of the Pacific War.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers","codebreaker biography"]},{"t":"The Rosetta Stone","u":"ciphers/rosetta-stone.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Ptolemaic Egypt · 196 BCE","b":"Solved 1822 (Champollion)","s":"The Rosetta Stone (196 BCE) — the trilingual decree that unlocked Egyptian hieroglyphs after fourteen centuries of silence, deciphered by Champollion in 1822.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","solved 1822 (champollion)"]},{"t":"ROT13","u":"ciphers/rot13.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1980s","b":"Trivial","s":"ROT13 (~1983, Usenet) — the self-inverse Caesar with shift 13, designed as a spoiler obscurer rather than a cipher. One operation toggles text in and out.","g":["19th century","world origins of cryptography","trivial"]},{"t":"RSA","u":"ciphers/rsa.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1977","b":"Secure (with 2048+ bit keys)","s":"The first practical public-key cryptosystem — one key encrypts, a different key decrypts, and you can publish the encrypting key in the phone book.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","secure (with 2048+ bit keys)"]},{"t":"Running Key Cipher","u":"ciphers/running-key.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"19th Century","b":"Weak","s":"Running Key cipher (19th c.) — a Vigenère variant using a long passage of natural text as the key. Broken by Friedman with high-probability-trigram dragging.","g":["19th century","polyalphabetic revolution","weak"]},{"t":"Sator Square","u":"ciphers/sator-square.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Roman Empire · 1st–4th c. CE","b":"Palindromic word square / hidden acrostic","s":"A 1st-century Roman five-letter palindromic word square — SATOR · AREPO · TENET · OPERA · ROTAS — readable forwards, backwards, up, and down. Every element of the square also rearranges into PATER NOSTER + A and O.","g":["ancient","cipher culture","palindromic word square / hidden acrostic"]},{"t":"Scytale","u":"ciphers/scytale.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Sparta · ~500 BC","b":"Broken","s":"Scytale (~500 BC) — the Spartan transposition device that wraps a leather strip around a rod of fixed diameter. Broken trivially by trying every rod width.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"SHA-256","u":"ciphers/sha256.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 2001","b":"Secure (no practical collision)","s":"SHA-256 (2001) — a 256-bit fingerprint for any input; the verification engine of Git, Bitcoin, TLS certificates, and software integrity worldwide.","g":["digital","modern cryptography","secure (no practical collision)"]},{"t":"Shamir's Secret Sharing","u":"ciphers/shamir-secret-sharing.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1979","b":"Information-theoretically secure","s":"Shamir's Secret Sharing (1979) — split a secret so any k of n holders can rebuild it and any k−1 learn absolutely nothing.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","information-theoretically secure"]},{"t":"Shugborough Inscription","u":"ciphers/shugborough.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Georgian England · ~1748–1763","b":"Unsolved monument inscription","s":"An 18th-century English garden monument bearing the cryptic letters O · U · O · S · V · A · V · V — debated for 250 years and never definitively cracked.","g":["modern","unsolved ciphers","unsolved monument inscription"]},{"t":"SIGABA (ECM Mark II)","u":"ciphers/sigaba.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Machines","c":"WWII · 1940","b":"Never broken in service","s":"SIGABA (ECM Mark II) (1940) — The American WWII rotor machine the Axis never broke — fifteen rotors, irregular stepping. Hall VII of The Cipher Museum.","g":["wwii","mechanical machines","never broken in service"]},{"t":"SIGSALY — The Encrypted Speech System","u":"ciphers/sigsaly.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWII · 1943–1946","b":"Unbroken","s":"SIGSALY (1943–1946) was the world's first perfectly secure voice encryption system — a 50-ton machine using vinyl phonograph records as one-time pads to protect Roosevelt-Churchill phone calls during WWII.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","unbroken"]},{"t":"Slidex","u":"ciphers/slidex.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"WWII & Cold War · 1943–1980s","b":"Tactical (intentionally weak)","s":"Slidex (1943) — the British Army's front-line tactical code card. A 204-cell vocabulary grid plus two sliding coordinate strips. Fast for a platoon signaller; read by German units within hours.","g":["wwii","civil war gallery","tactical (intentionally weak)"]},{"t":"Solitaire / Pontifex","u":"ciphers/solitaire.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"Modern · 1999","b":"Moderate","s":"Solitaire / Pontifex (1999) — Bruce Schneier's hand cipher driven by a 54-card deck (written for Cryptonomicon). Crowley showed a ~1/22.5 keystream bias.","g":["digital","the unbreakable","moderate"]},{"t":"Somerton Man Code","u":"ciphers/somerton-man.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Post-WWII Australia · 1948","b":"Unsolved Cold-War-era inscription","s":"An unidentified man found dead on an Adelaide beach in 1948 with the word 'Tamám Shud' in his pocket — and a page of letters that may or may not be a cipher.","g":["wwii","unsolved ciphers","unsolved cold-war-era inscription"]},{"t":"Stager Cipher","u":"ciphers/stager.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Union · 1861","b":"Weak","s":"Stager cipher (1861) — Anson Stager's Union Army route cipher that transposes whole words and salts them with code names. Never broken in the Civil War.","g":["wwi","civil war gallery","weak"]},{"t":"Station HYPO — Pearl Harbor to Midway","u":"ciphers/station-hypo.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · 1940–1942","b":"Broken (JN-25)","s":"Station HYPO at Pearl Harbor was the US Navy signals intelligence unit that broke JN-25 in 1942 and gave Admiral Nimitz advance warning of the Japanese fleet's target at Midway — turning the tide of the Pacific War.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers","broken (jn-25)"]},{"t":"Straddling Checkerboard","u":"ciphers/straddling-checkerboard.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Late 19th – 20th Century","b":"Weak (alone) · Strong (in VIC)","s":"Straddling Checkerboard (1930s) — the Soviet variable-length digit substitution: common letters get one digit, rare letters two. The substitution layer of VIC.","g":["19th century","military & spy ciphers","weak (alone) · strong (in vic)"]},{"t":"Tap Code","u":"ciphers/tap-code.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"Korean War · Vietnam War","b":"Broken","s":"Tap Code (1965) — the 5x5 Polybius square tapped through walls as row-then-column knocks. Used by US POWs in the Hanoi Hilton during the Vietnam War.","g":["cold war","puzzle & novelty ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Trifid Cipher","u":"ciphers/trifid.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"1902","b":"Broken","s":"Trifid cipher (1902) — Felix Delastelle's three-dimensional 3x3x3 extension of the Bifid that fractionates letters into trits and shuffles them across a period.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Trithemius Progressive Cipher","u":"ciphers/trithemius.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Polyalphabetic Era","c":"German Renaissance · 1518","b":"Easy","s":"Johannes Trithemius's progressive shift cipher (Polygraphia, 1518) — the first published polyalphabetic, where every successive letter is shifted one further than the last.","g":["renaissance","the polyalphabetic era","easy"]},{"t":"Two-Square Cipher","u":"ciphers/two-square.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Late 19th Century · ~1902","b":"Weak","s":"Two-Square cipher (late 19th c.) — a lighter Delastelle variant using only two keyed 5x5 squares. Many digrams self-encode, leaking the key squares.","g":["19th century","classical substitution","weak"]},{"t":"Typex","u":"ciphers/typex.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Machines","c":"WWII · 1937","b":"Never confirmed broken; far stronger than Enigma in service","s":"Typex (1937) — Britain's improved Enigma — five rotors, no plugboard, used at Bletchley to read its own intercepts. Hall VII of The Cipher Museum.","g":["wwii","mechanical machines","never confirmed broken; far stronger than enigma in service"]},{"t":"VENONA Project","u":"ciphers/venona.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"Cold War · 1943–1980","b":"Partial break (key reuse exploit)","s":"VENONA (1943–1980) was the US Army codebreaking effort that decrypted thousands of Soviet intelligence cables by exploiting key reuse in one-time pads — revealing Julius Rosenberg, Kim Philby, and the Cambridge Five.","g":["wwii","the unbreakable","partial break (key reuse exploit)"]},{"t":"Vernam Cipher","u":"ciphers/vernam.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"1917","b":"Weak (if key reused)","s":"Vernam cipher (1917) — Gilbert Vernam's AT&T XOR teleprinter patent, the direct ancestor of the One-Time Pad and every modern stream cipher including ChaCha20.","g":["wwi","the unbreakable","weak (if key reused)"]},{"t":"VIC Cipher","u":"ciphers/vic.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1950s","b":"Weak","s":"VIC cipher (1953) — Reino Häyhänen's KGB hand cipher: straddling checkerboard, double transposition, date-driven keystream. Broken in 1957 after his defection.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","weak"]},{"t":"Vietnamese Underground Codes","u":"ciphers/vietnamese-underground.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Modern · 1940s–1975","b":"Partially broken","s":"The Viet Minh and NLF used a system of keyed monoalphabetic substitution and prearranged signal codes during the Indochina and Vietnam Wars (1940s–1975).","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","partially broken"]},{"t":"Vigenère Cipher","u":"ciphers/vigenere.html","k":"cipher","h":"Hall III","c":"Renaissance · 1553","b":"Broken 1863","s":"Vigenère cipher (1553) — the polyalphabetic shift driven by a repeating keyword, called le chiffre indéchiffrable for 300 years. Broken by Kasiski in 1863.","g":["renaissance","hall iii","broken 1863"]},{"t":"The Voynich Manuscript","u":"ciphers/voynich.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Early 15th c. · Carbon-dated 1404–1438","b":"—","s":"Voynich Manuscript (~1400s) — a 240-page illustrated codex in an unknown script (Beinecke MS 408). Has resisted cipher and linguistic analysis for 600 years.","g":["renaissance","unsolved ciphers"]},{"t":"Wadsworth Cipher","u":"ciphers/wadsworth-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"1817 · USA","b":"Broken","s":"Wadsworth's cipher device (1817) — Colonel Decius Wadsworth's two geared disks, 33 outer positions against 26 inner letters, the first working progressive cipher machine. Broken by a ciphertext-only hill-climbing attack in 2020.","g":["19th century","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Wallis Ciphers","u":"ciphers/wallis-ciphers.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"English Civil War · 1642–1651","b":"Royalist nomenclators — broken by Wallis","s":"John Wallis of Oxford broke Royalist nomenclators for Parliament during the English Civil War, then served every English government for 50 years. Originator of professional English state cryptanalysis.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","royalist nomenclators — broken by wallis"]},{"t":"Wheatstone Cryptograph","u":"ciphers/wheatstone.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Victorian London · 1867","b":"Weak (1867 standards)","s":"Charles Wheatstone's 1867 cryptograph — two geared clock dials whose hands sweep an outer plain alphabet and an inner mixed alphabet, generating a polyalphabetic ciphertext one click at a time.","g":["victorian","mechanical cipher machines","weak (1867 standards)"]},{"t":"Herbert Yardley","u":"ciphers/yardley.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Interwar · USA","b":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Herbert Osborne Yardley founded America's Black Chamber in 1919, broke Japanese diplomatic codes at the 1921 Washington Naval Conference, and then infamously published the secrets in his 1931 bestseller.","g":["interwar","hall of codebreakers","codebreaker biography"]},{"t":"Zero-Knowledge Proofs","u":"ciphers/zero-knowledge-proofs.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1985","b":"Secure (foundational result)","s":"Zero-knowledge proofs (1985) — convince someone a statement is true while revealing nothing beyond its truth. Run the Schnorr protocol and its simulator.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","secure (foundational result)"]},{"t":"Zimmermann Telegram","u":"ciphers/zimmermann.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"WWI · January 1917","b":"Broken (Room 40)","s":"The decoded German telegram of January 1917 that brought the United States into WWI. A two-stage codebook cipher (0075 then 13040), broken by Britain’s Room 40 and surfaced through one of history’s most consequential intelligence operations.","g":["wwi","civil war gallery","broken (room 40)"]},{"t":"Zodiac Cipher","u":"ciphers/zodiac.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"1969 · Solved 2020","b":"Solved 2020","s":"Zodiac killer ciphers (1969) — homophonic substitution as in Z-408 (broken in 1969 by the Hardens) and Z-340 (broken in 2020 by Oranchak, Blake, and Van Eycke).","g":["cold war","puzzle & novelty ciphers","solved 2020"]},{"t":"World Origins of Cryptography","u":"halls/ancient.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall I · World Origins · ~1900 BCE - ~1350 CE","b":"—","s":"World origins of cryptography from ancient Egypt and Hebrew Atbash through Greek, Roman, Indian, and Arabic traditions.","g":["hall","ancient"]},{"t":"The American Civil War Gallery","u":"halls/civil-war.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall VI · Special Exhibition · 1861 – 1865","b":"—","s":"Civil War cryptography: the Stager cipher, Confederate Vigenère, and dictionary codes from the American Civil War.","g":["hall"]},{"t":"Hall of Codebreakers","u":"halls/codebreakers.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall X · The Human Stories","b":"—","s":"Twenty-one moments when human ingenuity broke what mathematics could not. From Al-Kindi (850 AD) to the Copiale and Chaocipher reconstructions of 2010-2014.","g":["hall","ancient","renaissance","19th century","wwi","cold war"]},{"t":"Cipher Culture","u":"halls/culture.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall XIII · Cipher Culture","b":"—","s":"Cryptography in art, music, literature, espionage fiction, and games — Bach's BACH motif, Poe's 'The Gold-Bug', Conan Doyle's Dancing Men, ARG ciphers, and modern puzzle hunts.","g":["hall","renaissance","19th century","modern","ancient"]},{"t":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","u":"halls/machines.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall VII · 1467 – 1945","b":"—","s":"Mechanical cipher machines: Enigma, Lorenz, Jefferson Disk, and Alberti’s original cipher disk.","g":["hall","renaissance","19th century","wwi","modern"]},{"t":"Military & Spy Ciphers","u":"halls/military.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall V · 1880s — Cold War · 1880 – 1970","b":"—","s":"Military and spy ciphers used in real warfare: Nihilist, VIC, Stager, and Confederate systems.","g":["hall","wwi","cold war","19th century"]},{"t":"Modern Cryptography","u":"halls/modern-crypto.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall XI · 1976 – Present","b":"—","s":"DES, Diffie-Hellman, RSA, AES, SHA-256 — the canonical five primitives that founded modern cryptography and anchor every secure system today.","g":["hall","modern","theoretical"]},{"t":"The Polyalphabetic Revolution","u":"halls/polyalphabetic.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall III · Renaissance — 19th Century · 1553 – 1880","b":"—","s":"The polyalphabetic revolution: Vigenère, Beaufort, Porta, and the ciphers that defeated simple frequency analysis.","g":["hall","renaissance","19th century"]},{"t":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","u":"halls/puzzle.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall VIII · 1605 – Present","b":"—","s":"Puzzle and novelty ciphers: Pigpen, Bacon’s bilateral, tap code, and other creative encoding systems.","g":["hall","medieval","renaissance","cold war","modern"]},{"t":"Classical Substitution","u":"halls/substitution.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall II · Medieval — Renaissance · 800 – 1929","b":"—","s":"Classical substitution ciphers: monoalphabetic, homophonic, and Polybius square techniques that shaped cryptography for centuries.","g":["hall","medieval","renaissance","19th century"]},{"t":"Transposition & Fractionation","u":"halls/transposition.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall IV · 19th – Early 20th Century","b":"—","s":"Transposition and fractionation ciphers: rail fence, columnar, bifid, trifid, and ADFGVX systems.","g":["hall","19th century","wwi"]},{"t":"The Unbreakable","u":"halls/unbreakable.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall IX · 1882 – Present","b":"—","s":"One-Time Pad, Vernam cipher, VENONA, and Claude Shannon's proof of perfect secrecy — where perfect theory met operational failure.","g":["hall","theoretical","wwi","19th century","cold war","modern"]},{"t":"Unsolved Ciphers","u":"halls/unsolved.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"Hall XII · Open Problems · Living Research","b":"—","s":"The world's most famous unbroken ciphers — Voynich, Kryptos, Beale, Dorabella, Zodiac Z-13/Z-32, Phaistos, Shugborough, D'Agapeyeff, Somerton Man, McCormick. Open invitations to cryptanalysts.","g":["hall","renaissance","modern","19th century","wwi","ancient"]},{"t":"The Closest Vector Problem","u":"hall-of-foundations/closest-vector.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§152","b":"—","s":"Round a target to the nearest lattice corner — right with a short basis, wrong with a skewed one. The decoding problem beneath lattice cryptography.","g":["foundations","mathematics","cvp","closest vector","lwe","learning with errors","kyber","ml-kem","babai"]},{"t":"Elliptic Curves over ℝ","u":"hall-of-foundations/elliptic-curves-real.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§150","b":"—","s":"A gentle first look at the elliptic-curve group law, drawn by hand, before it moves to a finite field.","g":["foundations","mathematics","elliptic curve","group law","chord tangent","ecc","ecdsa"]},{"t":"Information & Entropy","u":"hall-of-foundations/entropy.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§151","b":"—","s":"Shannon’s measure of surprise: why the one-time pad is perfect and why key length is destiny.","g":["foundations","mathematics","entropy","shannon","information theory","perfect secrecy","one-time pad"]},{"t":"Euler & Fermat","u":"hall-of-foundations/euler-fermat.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§145","b":"—","s":"The little theorem that makes RSA decryption undo RSA encryption. Watch a message round-trip.","g":["foundations","mathematics","euler","fermat","totient","rsa","little theorem"]},{"t":"Finite Fields","u":"hall-of-foundations/finite-fields.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§142","b":"—","s":"Watch a smooth elliptic curve shatter into a scatter of dots over a finite field — and see why the discrete logarithm becomes hard.","g":["foundations","mathematics","finite field","galois field","gf","discrete log","ecc"]},{"t":"Group Theory","u":"hall-of-foundations/group-theory.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§149","b":"—","s":"Generators, order, and cyclic structure — the grammar of the discrete-log family.","g":["foundations","mathematics","group theory","cyclic group","generator","order","diffie-hellman"]},{"t":"Hall of Foundations","u":"hall-of-foundations/index.html","k":"hall","h":"—","c":"—","b":"—","s":"The Hall of Foundations — interactive exhibits on the mathematics under cryptography: the number theory, hardness assumptions, and structures behind RSA, ECC, and the post-quantum standards.","g":["foundations","mathematics","hall"]},{"t":"Polynomial Interpolation · Hall of Foundations","u":"hall-of-foundations/interpolation.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§155","b":"—","s":"Drag points until the curve locks and the secret intercept appears — the heart of Shamir’s Secret Sharing.","g":["foundations","mathematics","interpolation","lagrange","shamir","secret sharing","threshold"]},{"t":"Lattices & the SVP","u":"hall-of-foundations/lattices-svp.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§147","b":"—","s":"Drag a basis and hunt the shortest vector — the lattice foundation under Kyber, Dilithium, and post-quantum cryptography.","g":["foundations","mathematics","lattice","svp","shortest vector","post-quantum","kyber","ml-kem"]},{"t":"Learning With Errors","u":"hall-of-foundations/learning-with-errors.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§157","b":"—","s":"Solve for a secret vector, then switch the noise on and watch the same algebra fail — the Learning With Errors problem beneath ML-KEM and ML-DSA.","g":["foundations","mathematics"]},{"t":"Modular Arithmetic","u":"hall-of-foundations/modular-arithmetic.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§143","b":"—","s":"Clock arithmetic, primitive roots, and the cyclic group Diffie–Hellman lives inside.","g":["foundations","mathematics","modular arithmetic","clock","primitive root","diffie-hellman"]},{"t":"The Ground Beneath the Curve","u":"hall-of-foundations/modularity-exhibit.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"—","b":"—","s":"The Ground Beneath the Curve — the modularity theorem and the number theory elliptic-curve cryptography inherits.","g":["foundations","mathematics","modularity theorem","fermat","wiles","number theory","ecc"]},{"t":"The One-Way Function","u":"hall-of-foundations/one-way-function.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§146","b":"—","s":"Multiply in a blink; factor and sweat — the one-way function, conceptual keystone of modern cryptography.","g":["foundations","mathematics","one-way function","factoring","trapdoor","hardness"]},{"t":"Bilinear Pairings · Hall of Foundations","u":"hall-of-foundations/pairings.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§156","b":"—","s":"Verify a product of hidden scalars without revealing them — the bilinear pairing under zk-SNARKs.","g":["foundations","mathematics","pairing","bilinear","zk-snark","zero-knowledge","ibe"]},{"t":"Polynomial Rings · Hall of Foundations","u":"hall-of-foundations/polynomial-rings.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§153","b":"—","s":"The polynomial ring R_q = Z_q[X]/(Xⁿ+1) — the structured lattice inside ML-KEM and ML-DSA.","g":["foundations","mathematics","polynomial ring","ntt","module-lwe","ml-kem","ml-dsa","post-quantum"]},{"t":"The Prime Number Theorem","u":"hall-of-foundations/prime-number-theorem.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§144","b":"—","s":"Why RSA can find a 2048-bit prime in moments — the Prime Number Theorem at work.","g":["foundations","mathematics","prime number theorem","primes","rsa","key generation"]},{"t":"The Random Oracle · Hall of Foundations","u":"hall-of-foundations/random-oracle.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§154","b":"—","s":"Flip one input bit, scramble half the output — the idealized hash assumption beneath Fiat–Shamir signatures.","g":["foundations","mathematics","random oracle","hash","avalanche","fiat-shamir","signatures"]},{"t":"Reduction & Proof","u":"hall-of-foundations/reduction.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Foundations","c":"§148","b":"—","s":"What “provably as hard as factoring” actually claims — and what it quietly assumes.","g":["foundations","mathematics","reduction","security proof","provable security","hardness"]},{"t":"Museum Map","u":"museum-map.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"Your Guide to the Collection","b":"—","s":"Navigate all 13 exhibit halls, the Hall of Foundations mathematics annex, and 160 exhibits in The Cipher Museum. Find your next cryptographic adventure.","g":[]},{"t":"History of Encryption","u":"timeline.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"3,900+ Years of Secrets","b":"—","s":"Visual history of encryption from c. 1900 BC to the quantum age. Walk through 3,900+ years of codes, ciphers, and codebreakers.","g":[]},{"t":"How Ciphers Work","u":"learn.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"Understanding Encryption","b":"—","s":"Understand how classical ciphers work and how they became the foundation for modern encryption — from Caesar's shift to AES.","g":[]},{"t":"Cryptanalysis Techniques","u":"cryptanalysis.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"Special Exhibit · The Science of Breaking Codes","b":"—","s":"Learn how codebreakers crack ciphers. Frequency analysis, Kasiski examination, index of coincidence, and modern computational methods.","g":[]},{"t":"Cipher Challenges","u":"challenges.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"Test Your Skills","b":"—","s":"Break real ciphers. Ten progressively harder challenges — from Caesar to Enigma.","g":[]},{"t":"Cryptography Glossary","u":"glossary.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"Reference Guide","b":"—","s":"Cryptography glossary: definitions of ciphers, cryptanalysis, key exchange, and encryption concepts from ancient to modern.","g":[]},{"t":"Modern Cryptography","u":"modern.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"Beyond the Museum","b":"—","s":"From AES and RSA to quantum cryptography — explore how modern encryption protects the digital world.","g":[]},{"t":"Cipher Comparison Table","u":"comparison.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"117 Cipher Systems from the 160-exhibit Collection","b":"—","s":"Compare 117 cipher systems from the 160-exhibit collection side by side. Sort by era, type, security level, hall, solved status, key method, and more.","g":[]},{"t":"Cipher Corpus","u":"cipher-corpus.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"Known-Ciphertext Benchmark Library","b":"—","s":"A curated library of ciphertext challenges, known plaintexts, keys, and metadata for learning, teaching, scoring, and benchmarking classical cryptanalysis.","g":["82 cipher types · 84 engines","9 languages","101 historical records","beginner → expert tiers","noisy & multilingual variants","jsonl / csv / json","llm 3-shot eval export"]},{"t":"Cipher Detective","u":"cipher-detective.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"Statistical Cryptanalysis","b":"—","s":"Paste unknown ciphertext and let the Cipher Detective walk you through the evidence — frequency analysis, Index of Coincidence, Kasiski examination — to identify the most likely cipher family.","g":[]},{"t":"Further Reading","u":"further-reading.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"Curator's Library","b":"—","s":"Curated bibliography for serious students of cryptography and cryptanalysis: canonical books, academic journals, scholar profiles, and primary-source archives.","g":[]},{"t":"Popular Culture Survey","u":"popular-culture-survey.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"Hall XIII Companion","b":"—","s":"Filterable survey of cipher references in novels, television, films, and internet puzzle culture.","g":[]},{"t":"Teaching & Overview","u":"teaching.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"Cipher Corpus","b":"—","s":"Teaching and overview page for the Cipher Corpus: learn about classical ciphers, cryptanalysis, and how to use the benchmark dataset.","g":[]},{"t":"My Cryptographer's Path — The Cipher Museum","u":"my-path.html","k":"page","h":"—","c":"—","b":"—","s":"Track your progress through the Cipher Museum learning path. View earned badges, quiz scores, and your journey through each hall.","g":[]}]}
