ne Cipher","u":"ciphers/affine.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Substitution Hall","c":"Antiquity → 1800s · math-age","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"","s":"The affine cipher — a single linear formula E(x)=a·x+b mod 26 that generalises Caesar (a=1) and Atbash (a=25, b=25). Just 312 keys; trivially broken.","g":["ancient","the substitution hall","broken"]},{"t":"Agnes Meyer Driscoll","u":"ciphers/agnes-driscoll.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Interwar & WWII · USA","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Agnes Meyer Driscoll was the U.S. Navy's chief cryptanalyst for three decades, breaking Japanese naval codes and training generations of American codebreakers. Known as 'Miss Aggie' and 'Madame X', she was the most formidable cryptanalyst in prewar America.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Alberti Cipher Disk","u":"ciphers/alberti-disk.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Renaissance · 1467","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Index of Coincidence · Frequency analysis","s":"Alberti cipher disk — Leon Battista Alberti's 1467 rotating disk, the first true polyalphabetic device and the tool that began the cipher revolution.","g":["renaissance","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Ethiopian Geʼez Monastic Ciphers","u":"ciphers/amharic-ge-ez-ciphers.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"East Africa · 14th–19th c.","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Historical (low security)","s":"Ethiopian Orthodox monasteries used syllabary-based substitution ciphers in Ge'ez script to protect sacred texts and inter-monastery communications from the 14th century onward.","g":["medieval","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Arabic Nomenclators","u":"ciphers/arabic-nomenclators.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Abbasid Caliphate · ~850 CE","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Broken (by its own inventors)","s":"9th-century Abbasid Baghdad — al-Kindi's Risalah and the codebooks of the Arab cryptographic golden age, where frequency analysis was born.","g":["medieval","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Argenti Family Cipher","u":"ciphers/argenti.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Renaissance Italy · 1500s–1600s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"","s":"Matteo and Marcello Argenti served as papal cryptanalysts to seven popes. Their <em>Trattato in Cifra</em> codified the homophonic-nomenclator pattern that dominated European diplomacy for two centuries.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Arnold–André Book Cipher","u":"ciphers/arnold-andre.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"American Revolution · 1779–1780","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Compromised by capture (1780)","s":"The book cipher Benedict Arnold and Major John André used to plot the surrender of West Point. Each plaintext word located in a shared book (Blackstone’s <em>Commentaries</em>) and transmitted as the triple page.line.word.","g":["modern","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Atbash","u":"ciphers/atbash.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Ancient · ~500 BC","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Inspection (it is its own inverse)","s":"Atbash — the ancient Hebrew reflection cipher (A<->Z, B<->Y, C<->X), self-inverse and used in the Book of Jeremiah (25:26, 51:41) for SHESHACH.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Autokey","u":"ciphers/autokey.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Frontier","c":"Renaissance · 1586","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Friedman's index of coincidence + autokey-aware crib dragging (1920s)","s":"Autokey (1586) — Vigenère's self-extending key, the message becomes its own key. Effectively unbreakable until Friedman's 1920s statistical attack.","g":["renaissance","polyalphabetic frontier","broken"]},{"t":"The Babington Plot Cipher","u":"ciphers/babington.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Elizabethan · 1586","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Thomas Phelippes, working for Sir Francis Walsingham · within days of interception","s":"Babington Plot nomenclator (1586) — the cipher that condemned Mary, Queen of Scots, broken in days by Walsingham's cryptographer Thomas Phelippes.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Bach's B-A-C-H Motif","u":"ciphers/bach-motif.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Baroque Germany · 1750","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Musical cryptogram","s":"The four notes B♭ – A – C – B♮ spell 'BACH' in German musical notation — a self-signature Bach embedded in his final unfinished fugue.","g":["modern","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Bacon's Cipher","u":"ciphers/bacon.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"1605","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Pattern recognition · Statistical analysis of A/B distribution","s":"Bacon's bilateral cipher (1605) — Francis Bacon's 5-bit binary code that hides letters inside a two-typeface carrier, the direct ancestor of ASCII.","g":["renaissance","puzzle & novelty ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Bazeries Cipher","u":"ciphers/bazeries.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"1890s · France","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Layered frequency analysis","s":"Bazeries cylinder cipher — Etienne Bazeries' 1891 wheel cipher with 20 alphabet disks, an evolution later adopted by the US Army as the M-94 in 1922.","g":["19th century","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Beale Ciphers","u":"ciphers/beale.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"19th Century · published 1885","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Unsolved (Beale 1 & 3)","s":"Beale ciphers — three numerical ciphers published in an 1885 Lynchburg pamphlet, said to have been written in the 1820s and to point to a Virginia treasure. Only #2 was solved (Declaration of Independence key); #1 and #3 unsolved.","g":["19th century","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Beaufort Cipher","u":"ciphers/beaufort.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"Victorian · 1857","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Kasiski Examination · Index of Coincidence","s":"Beaufort cipher (1857) — Sir Francis Beaufort's reciprocal Vigenère variant where encrypt and decrypt are the same operation. Broken by Kasiski examination.","g":["19th century","polyalphabetic revolution","broken"]},{"t":"Arne Beurling","u":"ciphers/beurling.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Sweden","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Swedish mathematician Arne Beurling broke the German Siemens T52 Geheimschreiber cipher in two weeks in 1940 — an achievement described by NSA historians as perhaps the greatest individual cryptanalytic feat in history.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Bible Code — Equidistant Letter Sequences","u":"ciphers/bible-code.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty Ciphers","c":"1994 · Refuted 1999","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Debunked","s":"The Bible Code — equidistant letter sequences (ELS) claimed to hide prophecies in the Hebrew scriptures. A rigorous statistical refutation by McKay et al. (1999) showed the same method finds identical 'predictions' in War and Peace and Moby-Dick.","g":["digital","puzzle & novelty ciphers"]},{"t":"Bifid Cipher","u":"ciphers/bifid.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"1901","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Statistical analysis · Hill climbing","s":"Bifid cipher (1901) — Felix Delastelle's fractionation cipher that splits Polybius coordinates, transposes them across a period, and recombines.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Bill Tutte","u":"ciphers/bill-tutte.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · Britain","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Bill Tutte was a Cambridge mathematician who reconstructed the complete logical structure of the Lorenz SZ40 cipher machine from ciphertext alone — without ever seeing the machine — enabling the creation of Colossus, the world's first programmable electronic computer.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Bletchley Park","u":"ciphers/bletchley-park.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWII · 1939–1945","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"","s":"Bletchley Park (Station X) was the British WWII signals intelligence headquarters where 10,000 people broke Enigma, Lorenz, and dozens of other Axis ciphers — arguably shortening the war by two years.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Book Cipher","u":"ciphers/book-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Field","c":"18th–20th c.","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Identifying the book — the Beale Cipher 2 was solved when the anonymous pamphleteer (published by James Ward, 1885) tried the Declaration of Independence","s":"Book Cipher (18th c.) — if both correspondents share the same book, the book itself is the key. Used by Benedict Arnold, Aaron Burr, and Beale.","g":["19th century","military & field","broken"]},{"t":"Cabinet Noir — Black Chambers of Europe","u":"ciphers/cabinet-noir.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Europe · 1550–1850","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"","s":"The European Black Chambers (cabinets noirs) were government departments that intercepted, deciphered, and resealed diplomatic mail from the 16th through 19th centuries — proto-signals-intelligence agencies.","g":["renaissance","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Caesar Cipher","u":"ciphers/caesar.html","k":"cipher","h":"Hall I","c":"Ancient Rome","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frequency Analysis · Al-Kindi · 850 AD","s":"Caesar cipher — the simplest substitution cipher, used by Julius Caesar around 58 BC with a shift of 3. Broken by Al-Kindi's frequency analysis circa 850 AD.","g":["ancient","hall i","broken"]},{"t":"Cardano Autokey","u":"ciphers/cardano-autokey.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Polyalphabetic Era","c":"Italian Renaissance · 1550","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Easy (recovery cascade)","s":"Girolamo Cardano's 1550 autokey — prime the keystream with a single seed letter, then continue it with the plaintext itself. The first published self-keying scheme.","g":["renaissance","the polyalphabetic era","broken"]},{"t":"Cardano Grille","u":"ciphers/cardano-grille.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Transposition Hall","c":"Italian Renaissance · 1550","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Weak (steganography, not encryption)","s":"Girolamo Cardano's 1550 grille — a card with cut-out windows. Write the secret through the windows; fill the rest with innocuous text; the recipient overlays an identical grille to read.","g":["renaissance","the transposition hall","historical only"]},{"t":"Chaocipher","u":"ciphers/chaocipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Modern · 1918","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Published key-recovery attacks by Lasry, Rubin, Kopal and Wacker (2016).","s":"Chaocipher (1918) — John F. Byrne's two-disk dynamic cipher. Disclosed in 2010; published known-plaintext and in-depth ciphertext-only attacks recover its keys.","g":["wwi","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Che Guevara's VIC Variant","u":"ciphers/che-guevara.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1956–1967","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Bolivian Army capture (1967)","s":"Ernesto 'Che' Guevara used a modified VIC straddling-checkerboard cipher for guerrilla communications in Bolivia and the Congo, 1956–1967.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"Chinese Telegraph Code","u":"ciphers/chinese-telegraph.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Late Qing → PRC · 1881–1980s","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Public codebook (super-enciphered when secret)","s":"The 1881 Chinese Telegraph Code mapped 7,000+ Chinese characters to 4-digit code groups so they could ride the same Morse-style telegraph network as the Latin alphabet — and was super-enciphered for diplomatic and military traffic in the 20th century.","g":["modern","civil war gallery"]},{"t":"Cicada 3301","u":"ciphers/cicada-3301.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Internet era · 2012–2014","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Multi-stage ARG / recruitment puzzle","s":"An anonymous 2012–2014 internet recruitment puzzle that combined steganography, classical ciphers, GPS coordinates on physical posters, and a copy of the Liber Primus hand-illustrated in glyphic runes.","g":["digital","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Columnar Transposition","u":"ciphers/columnar.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Frequency analysis · Anagramming","s":"Columnar transposition — write plaintext under a keyword, then read columns in keyword order. Broken by Friedman-era multiple-anagram methods in the 1920s.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Commercial Telegraph Codebooks","u":"ciphers/commercial-codebooks.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Victorian → inter-war · 1870s–1930s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Public codebooks (compression, not secrecy)","s":"Commercial telegraph codebooks (ABC Code, Bentley’s, Lieber’s) were the workhorses of Victorian and Edwardian global business. Each English word or phrase mapped to a 5-letter pronounceable codeword — cheaper, denser, and incidentally a layer of confidentiality.","g":["modern","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Confederate Vigenère","u":"ciphers/confederate-vigenere.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Confederate · 1862","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Union cryptanalysts · Kasiski (published 1863)","s":"Confederate Vigenère (1861-1865) — the brass cipher disk used by Confederate officers, broken in 1863 once the three repeating field keys were recovered.","g":["wwi","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Copiale Cipher","u":"ciphers/copiale.html","k":"exhibit","h":"Puzzle & Novelty","c":"18th Century · ~1760s","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Knight, Megyesi, Schaefer (USC/Uppsala, 2011)","s":"Copiale Cipher — the 105-page 18th-century Oculist Order manuscript with homophonic substitution. Broken in 2011 by Knight, Megyesi, and Schaefer using HMM.","g":["renaissance","puzzle & novelty","broken"]},{"t":"Culper Ring / Tallmadge Code","u":"ciphers/culper-ring.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"American Revolution · 1778–1783","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Codebook (never captured, never broken)","s":"Major Benjamin Tallmadge’s 1779 codebook for George Washington’s Culper Spy Ring. Roughly 760 numbered entries covering people, places, military terms, and common words — used by Abraham Woodhull and Robert Townsend on British-occupied Long Island and Manhattan.","g":["modern","military & spy ciphers","broken"]},{"t":"The Da Vinci Code","u":"ciphers/da-vinci-code.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Fiction · 2003","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Pop Culture","s":"Dan Brown's 2003 thriller made cryptography mainstream — Atbash, the Fibonacci sequence, and anagrams as plot devices that introduced millions to cipher thinking.","g":["digital","cipher culture"]},{"t":"D'Agapeyeff Cipher","u":"ciphers/dagapeyeff.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"Pre-WWII Britain · 1939","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Unsolved challenge cipher","s":"A 1939 textbook 'cryptogram for the student' that the author himself later admitted he had forgotten how to solve.","g":["wwii","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Conan Doyle's Dancing Men","u":"ciphers/dancing-men.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Edwardian Britain · 1903","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Fictional substitution cipher","s":"The fictional stick-figure cipher Sherlock Holmes solves in The Adventure of the Dancing Men (1903) — a frequency-analysis primer disguised as a Strand magazine story.","g":["modern","cipher culture"]},{"t":"DES (Data Encryption Standard)","u":"ciphers/des.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1977","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Broken (brute force, 1998)","s":"DES (1977) — the first public, government-standardised cipher; broken by EFF Deep Crack in 1998 and the spark of modern academic cryptanalysis.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","broken"]},{"t":"Diana Cryptosystem","u":"ciphers/diana-cryptosystem.html","k":"cipher","h":"Military & Spy Ciphers","c":"Cold War · 1960s–1990s","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"","s":"The US Army Special Forces one-time pad field system (1960s–1990s) — trigraph-based OTP cards issued to Green Berets in Vietnam.","g":["cold war","military & spy ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Confederate Dictionary Code","u":"ciphers/dictionary-code.html","k":"cipher","h":"Civil War Gallery","c":"Confederate · 1862","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Known dictionary + position lookup","s":"Dictionary code — the Civil War book cipher that encodes each word as page-and-word-number pairs keyed to a shared dictionary, immune to frequency attacks.","g":["wwi","civil war gallery","broken"]},{"t":"Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange","u":"ciphers/diffie-hellman.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1976","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"","s":"Diffie-Hellman (1976) — two strangers create a shared secret over an open wire, ending three thousand years of must-meet-first cryptography.","g":["cold war","modern cryptography","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Dilly Knox","u":"ciphers/dilly-knox.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"WWI/WWII · Britain","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Alfred Dillwyn \"Dilly\" Knox was the British codebreaker who solved the Enigma wiring before Turing, broke the Abwehr Enigma, and was the first to crack the Italian commercial Enigma variant.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Dorabella Cipher","u":"ciphers/dorabella.html","k":"unsolved","h":"Unsolved Ciphers","c":"19th c. · 1897","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Nobody — over 125 years of attempts, no consensus solution","s":"Dorabella Cipher (1897) — Edward Elgar's 1897 cryptogram — 87 squiggles that have resisted every attempt since. Hall XII of The Cipher Museum.","g":["19th century","unsolved ciphers","historical only"]},{"t":"Double Transposition","u":"ciphers/double-transposition.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"WWI · Germany","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Anagramming · Hill climbing (difficult)","s":"Double transposition — apply columnar transposition twice with two keys. The WWII Allied workhorse, considered unbreakable until Lasry et al. (2013).","g":["wwi","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Elonka Dunin","u":"ciphers/dunin.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Modern · Cryptographer","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Living Legend","s":"Elonka Dunin is a game designer, cryptographer, and the foremost public authority on unsolved ciphers — the creator of the definitive list of famous unsolved codes and an expert on Kryptos.","g":["modern","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"ECDSA","u":"ciphers/ecdsa.html","k":"cipher","h":"Modern Cryptography","c":"Modern · 1992","b":"Secure (Modern)","trust":{"key":"secure","text":"Secure (Modern)","description":"Still considered secure when used correctly with modern key handling."},"n":"","s":"ECDSA (1992) — signing with elliptic curves, and the one-line mistake that handed Sony's PlayStation 3 master key to the world.","g":["digital","modern cryptography","secure (modern)"]},{"t":"Egyptian Substitution Hieroglyphs","u":"ciphers/egyptian-substitution.html","k":"cipher","h":"World Origins of Cryptography","c":"Middle Kingdom Egypt · ~1900 BCE","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Decorative concealment","s":"The Khnumhotep II tomb inscription (~1900 BCE) — the world's earliest surviving instance of a deliberate substitution cipher, using unusual hieroglyph variants.","g":["ancient","world origins of cryptography"]},{"t":"Elizebeth Smith Friedman","u":"ciphers/elizebeth-friedman.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Interwar & WWII · USA","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Codebreaker Biography","s":"Elizebeth Smith Friedman was America's first female professional cryptanalyst, who broke rum-runner codes during Prohibition, exposed Nazi spy networks in South America, and laid the foundations for what became the NSA.","g":["wwii","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Enigma Machine","u":"ciphers/enigma.html","k":"cipher","h":"The Unbreakable","c":"WWII · 1918–1945","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Bombe machine · Crib attacks · Alan Turing · Bletchley Park","s":"Enigma (1923) — the German rotor cipher securing Wehrmacht and Kriegsmarine traffic. Broken by Polish mathematician Marian Rejewski in 1932.","g":["wwii","the unbreakable","broken"]},{"t":"Fialka M-125","u":"ciphers/fialka.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"Cold War · 1956","b":"Historical Only","trust":{"key":"historical","text":"Historical Only","description":"No longer in use, or never confirmed broken/deciphered."},"n":"Never publicly broken by cryptanalysis in operational service; obsolete today.","s":"The Soviet Cold-War rotor machine. Ten rotors, half stepping forward and half backward, plus a punch-card key sheet — the answer to every weakness Soviet analysts saw in Enigma.","g":["cold war","mechanical cipher machines","historical only"]},{"t":"Field Hollers and Coding Songs","u":"ciphers/field-hollers.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Americas · 1619–1865","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Historical (social steganography)","s":"American enslaved workers used field hollers, work songs, and coded idiom as covert communication — \"Follow the Drinking Gourd\" encoding an escape route along the Underground Railroad.","g":["modern","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Four-Square Cipher","u":"ciphers/four-square.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Late 19th Century · ~1902","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Digram frequency analysis · simulated annealing","s":"Four-Square cipher (1902) — Delastelle's digram cipher using two keyed and two standard 5x5 squares. Disrupts Playfair frequencies but breaks to digrams.","g":["19th century","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Fractionated Morse Cipher","u":"ciphers/fractionated-morse.html","k":"cipher","h":"Transposition & Fractionation","c":"19th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Structural analysis of Morse patterns","s":"Fractionated Morse — converts text to Morse, regroups dots, dashes, and separators in trigrams, then substitutes via a keyed alphabet. Broken by trigram stats.","g":["19th century","transposition & fractionation","broken"]},{"t":"Freemason Pigpen Tradition","u":"ciphers/freemason-pigpen.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Early modern Europe · 17th–19th c.","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Fraternal symbol-substitution alphabet","s":"The geometric pigpen alphabet — fragments of grids and X-shapes — used by Freemasons, Rosicrucians, and the Hellfire Club from the 17th century onwards as a fraternal cipher and ritual marker.","g":["modern","cipher culture"]},{"t":"The GCHQ Trio — Ellis, Cocks, and Williamson","u":"ciphers/gchq-trio.html","k":"person","h":"Hall of Codebreakers","c":"Cold War · GCHQ","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Invention Biography","s":"James Ellis, Clifford Cocks, and Malcolm Williamson of GCHQ independently invented public-key cryptography in 1970–1973 — but their work remained classified until 1997, some twenty years after Diffie, Hellman, and Rivest had received public credit.","g":["cold war","hall of codebreakers"]},{"t":"Geheimschreiber T52 (Sturgeon)","u":"ciphers/geheimschreiber.html","k":"cipher","h":"Mechanical Cipher Machines","c":"WWII · 1932–1945","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Broken (Beurling, 1940)","s":"The Siemens T52 “Sturgeon” — a German WWII teleprinter cipher with ten irregularly-clocked wheels feeding an additive keystream and a per-character permutation. Broken by Arne Beurling and the Swedish FRA in summer 1940.","g":["wwii","mechanical cipher machines","broken"]},{"t":"Poe's Gold-Bug Cipher","u":"ciphers/gold-bug.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Antebellum America · 1843","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Fictional substitution cipher","s":"Edgar Allan Poe's 1843 short story The Gold-Bug — the first widely read piece of fiction to teach cryptanalysis, complete with a worked decipherment of a buried-treasure cryptogram.","g":["modern","cipher culture"]},{"t":"Gravity Falls Cipher System","u":"ciphers/gravity-falls.html","k":"cipher","h":"Cipher Culture","c":"Animation · 2012–2016","b":"—","trust":null,"n":"Pop Culture","s":"The Disney animated series Gravity Falls (2012–2016) hid a different cipher in every episode's credits — Caesar, Atbash, A1Z26 numeric, Vigenère — teaching real cryptography to a generation of children.","g":["digital","cipher culture"]},{"t":"The Great Cipher","u":"ciphers/great-cipher.html","k":"cipher","h":"Classical Substitution","c":"Baroque · ~1626","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Étienne Bazeries (1893): syllabic frequency analysis and contextual cribs.","s":"Great Cipher of Louis XIV (1626) — the Rossignol father-and-son syllabic nomenclator with delete-previous-letter traps. Broken by Bazeries in 1893.","g":["renaissance","classical substitution","broken"]},{"t":"Gronsfeld Cipher","u":"ciphers/gronsfeld.html","k":"cipher","h":"Polyalphabetic Revolution","c":"17th Century","b":"Broken","trust":{"key":"broken","text":"Broken","description":"Cryptographically broken — historically important but unsafe to use today."},"n":"Kasiski · Only 10 possible shifts per position","s":"Gronsfeld cipher (1655) — a Vigenère variant